# mini_agent Project Conventions
# Read this before making changes. Consult STATE.txt for current architecture decisions.
#
# NOTE: This file contains the full AI behavioral instructions — formerly in
# prompt.py. The system prompt is now pi-style ultra-minimal (~200 tokens).
# All detailed behavioral rules, edit policies, loop prevention, etc. live HERE
# and are appended at the END of the system prompt, outside the cache prefix.
#
# SELF-MODIFICATION: This agent modifies its own codebase. After every
# completed change, update STATE.txt. At session end, write HANDOFF.md.
# Log significant changes in CHANGELOG.md with date and reason.

## Code Style
- Use type hints on all public functions. Prefer dataclasses for structured data.
- No magic numbers — define named UPPER_CASE constants in the relevant module.
- Keep modules small and single-purpose. Use explicit control flow over magic.
- No circular imports. No global mutable state unless unavoidable.
- All tool results must be structured dataclasses — never raw exceptions.
- Use `from __future__ import annotations` in every module.

## Module Map
# core/                    — core orchestration
#   constants.py           — shared constants (no project imports, safe for any module)
#   prompt.py              — system prompt, personality rules
#   config.py              — AgentConfig (TOML + env + CLI), startup context
#   llm.py                 — API calls, turn orchestration, tool piping
#   safety.py              — workspace read/write gates (SafetyResult dataclass)
#   bootstrap.py           — session init, workspace setup
#   context_inject.py      — per-turn context injection (handoff, state, git diff, etc.)
#   codebase_map.py        — AST-based symbol extraction for startup context
#   knowledge_graph.py     — entity-relationship graph (calls, imports, defs)
#   tree_sitter_parser.py  — multi-language source parsing (Python/JS/TS)
# memory/                  — SQLite persistence
#   memory.py              — MemoryStore: conversations, knowledge, scratchpad
#   memory_prune.py        — content-aware compression, orphan stripping
#   session.py             — session lifecycle
# agents/                  — multi-agent orchestration
#   agent_runtime.py       — sub-agent lifecycle, inboxes, file reservations
#   sub_agent.py           — sub-agent engine, turn budget, pruning, Pro model
# api.py                   — LLM API calls, message cache
# interject.py             — thread-safe user interjection queue
# terminal.py              — ANSI colour helpers
# retry.py                 — HTTP retry with jitter and backoff
# stream.py                — SSE stream parser
# logging_setup.py         — structured logging
# discord_bot.py           — Discord bot (emotion game + workspace assistant)
# voice_handler.py         — TTS voice synthesis (ElevenLabs, macOS say)
# workspace_bot.py         — workspace assistant bot entry point
# tools/                   — tool implementations
#   __init__.py            — dispatch, cache, ToolResult, JSON repair
#   schema.py              — TOOLS definitions sent to LLM
#   file_ops.py            — read/write/edit/list/info/scratchpad/diff/plan
#   shell_ops.py           — run_shell, run_tests, search_files, git, task_status, verify
#   search_ops.py          — find_symbol, find_usages, semantic_search, web_search, recall_turn
#   agent_ops.py           — extend, cancel, wait, restore, session_stats, recall_turn, remember, read_image
#   agent_spawn.py         — sub-agent spawning (spawn_agent, _spawn_one)
#   agent_collect.py       — sub-agent status & collection (agent_status, collect_agent, collect_any)
#   agent_messages.py      — typed message registry, validation, routing
#   agent_patterns.py      — fan_out, fan_in, pipeline, barrier, scatter_gather
#   agent_todos.py         — per-agent todo tracking, plan, write_scratchpad
#   result.py              — ToolResult dataclass
#   context.py             — AgentContext, _ContextProxy, _TOOL_CONTEXT
#   reservations.py        — file reservation system for sub-agent write collision prevention
#   skills.py              — lazy tool loading via skill gates (11 core tools, 11 skill groups)
#   error_hints.py         — error hint generation and failure fingerprinting
#   failure_learning.py    — failure pattern store, self-critique, project knowledge
#   tool_graph.py          — tool transition graph for sequencing hints
#   lsp.py                 — LSP client (pylsp integration)
#   mcp_client.py          — MCP client (stdio JSON-RPC tool discovery)
#   _json_rpc_shared.py    — shared drain_stderr() for LSP and MCP clients
#   desktop_ops.py         — desktop automation (macOS Atomacos, Windows UIA)
#   macos_ops.py           — macOS-specific tools (apps, windows, clipboard, keys)
#   browser_ops.py         — Playwright headless browser automation
#   semantic_cache.py      — semantic response cache (cosine-sim match, 15-25% cost reduction)
#   memory_consolidation.py — background knowledge consolidation (extract facts, apply to core)
#   memory_core.py         — core memory operations (read, add, replace, remove)
# skills/                  — Hermes-style skill definitions (SKILL.md with YAML frontmatter)
# conftest.py              — shared test fixtures and mocks
# STATE.txt                — architecture decisions, current state (consult before major changes)
# HANDOFF.md               — session handoff (read at startup, write at session end)
# CHANGELOG.md             — self-modification audit trail (update after significant changes)
# TASKS.md                 — task-to-file mapping index

## Testing
- Test files live in `tests/` directory (e.g. `tests/test_memory.py`). Use unittest.TestCase.
- Tests with supporting files (mock servers, fixtures) may live in subdirectories of `tests/`.
- Run relevant tests after every code change (use `run_tests` tool with path).
- ~1100 tests currently, all must pass. Do not stack speculative fixes — verify each.
- Use tempfile.mkdtemp for test workspaces; clean up in tearDown.

## Logging
- Logs live outside workspace at `~/.mini_agent/logs/`:
  - `agent.log` — all tool calls, JSON-lines, rotating 5×10MB
  - `api_error.log` — LLM API errors (HTTP status, rate limits)
  - `error_traces.log` — full tracebacks for crashes
- Entry point: `logging_setup.py` (see `LOG_DIR`, `get_logger()`, `log_tool_failure()`)
- Run `tail -50 ~/.mini_agent/logs/agent.log | python3 -m json.tool` to inspect recent events

## Architecture Rules
- Before touching >3 core files, pause and explain the plan first.
- Reuse existing abstractions before introducing new ones. Avoid duplicate logic.
- Do not create new subsystems unless existing architecture cannot handle the need.
- Prefer small incremental edits over large rewrites.
- Prefer readable code over clever code. Add TODO comments, not partial systems.

## Preferred Patterns
- Use `find_symbol` / `read_file` to consult existing code before changes (never guess).
- Use LSP tools (`lsp_definition`, `lsp_references`, `lsp_hover`, `lsp_diagnostics`) for
  precise code intelligence — go-to-definition, find-all-references, type hovering, and
  diagnostics. Prefer these over grep for understanding code structure.
- Before non-trivial tasks, state your plan in 1-3 sentences.
- Make parallel independent tool calls in one batch; only serialize when dependent.
- Use `write_scratchpad` to track progress across turns.
- Update STATE.txt after every completed change.
- Every new feature needs at least one test.
- If tests fail, stop and diagnose before making additional changes.

## Tool Usage Rules
- NEVER use '?', '???', '...', or empty strings as tool parameter values. These are
  placeholder markers that the runtime automatically refuses with ✗ REFUSED.
- If you don't know a path, content, or argument value: use discovery tools
  (list_directory, search_files, read_file, find_symbol) to find the real value.
- Guessing with '?' wastes a turn and triggers storm-breaker protection.

## Git Operations
- Use `use_skill('git')` to unlock git_status, git_diff, git_log, git_add, git_commit.
- These tools are Windows-safe (GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, 15s timeout) — they never hang.
- Do NOT use `run_shell` for git commands — it can hang on Windows.
- Use `git_status` before changes, `git_diff` to review, `git_add` + `git_commit` to save.
- For push: use `run_shell(command='git push', ...)` (blocked without force=True).


## Self-Review Cycle
After completing a significant change (new file, refactored module, or >50 lines edited):
1. **Observe**: what did I just do? What files did I touch?
2. **Diagnose**: did I encounter any new failure modes, edge cases, or patterns?
3. **Improve**: should this be crystallized as a project rule, a knowledge entry, or a fix strategy?
4. **Verify**: run relevant tests. If they fail, stop and diagnose before continuing.
5. **Document**: update STATE.txt (architecture), HANDOFF.md (session), CHANGELOG.md (audit).

At the end of every session, write a HANDOFF.md with:
- What I changed (files and reasoning)
- What's pending (unfinished work)
- Modified files list (for git context on next startup)


## AI Behavioral Rules
These are the PRIMARY behavioral instructions. The system prompt is minimal (~200 tokens)
to maximize LLM prompt cache hit rates. All detailed rules live here.

### Knowledge & Search
- Rate your confidence 1-10 on external knowledge questions.
  1-6: use web_search FIRST, don't guess.
  7-8: fairly sure, verify if high-consequence.
  9-10: answer directly.
- Codebase-first: search local code before web_search. But if local search misses, web_search promptly.
- 3+ consecutive tool failures → web_search, don't keep retrying.

### Loop Prevention (CRITICAL)
- Same tool + same args 2x = STUCK. Switch approach immediately.
- Long commands (>10s): use background mode.
- read_file MUST be called before edit_file/write_file on the same file.
- write_file creates new files OR overwrites existing files. For small targeted
  changes to an existing file, PREFER edit_file with oldText/newText. For full
  rewrites, write_file is appropriate. NEVER delete a file first — just use
  write_file directly on the path you want.
- Preferred: pi-style oldText/newText editing — no anchors needed.
  Example: edits:[{oldText:'print("hello")', newText:'print("hi")'}]
- For insertions: edit_type='insert_after' with oldText as anchor line.
  Example: edits:[{oldText:'import os', newText:'import sys', edit_type:'insert_after'}]
- NEVER write Python scripts to edit files. NEVER use sed -i. NEVER use shell
  redirects to modify workspace files. Only edit_file or write_file.

### Windows Commands
- Git Bash on Windows. Single quotes work: python -c 'code'
- For multi-line Python: write_file then run_shell.
- Pipe/filter: run_shell(command='head -n 50 file.py')

### Read-Before-Edit (ACI Guardrails)
- CANNOT write/edit .py files not read_file'd this session.
- After edit_file/write_file, verify with syntax check or test.
- Shell exit 0 with no output = normal, not error.
- Search capped at 200 results; narrow pattern if at cap.
- Dangerous commands blocked (rm -rf, sudo, git push --force) unless force=True.

### Token Efficiency
- read_file output capped at 2000 lines/50KB. Use offset/limit.
- run_shell output tail-truncated to 2000 lines/50KB; full output saved to temp file.
- Batch ALL independent tool calls in ONE response.
- Use get_file_skeleton BEFORE read_file for structure (5-20x savings).
- Prefer find_symbol over search_files (10x faster).
- Update write_scratchpad every 3 turns.

### Plan-before-Edit
- Changes spanning 3+ files or 50+ lines: call plan() FIRST.
- Plan: files, order, verification per file.
- Mark steps with plan_status(step=N).
- Stuck without a plan? STOP and create one.

### AST-Native Tools
- get_file_skeleton: structural outline (signatures only, no bodies). Use BEFORE read_file.
- get_function: extract specific function by name (supports ClassName.method).
- replace_symbol: AST-precise function/method/class replacement.

### Placeholder Guard
- Runtime refuses '?', '???', '...', empty strings as ANY parameter.
- Use discovery tools (list_directory, search_files, read_file, find_symbol) instead.

### Multi-Agent (use_skill('agents') first)
- spawn_agent for independent sub-tasks. Patterns: fan_out, fan_in, pipeline, barrier.
- Orchestrate, don't duplicate.

### Session Handoff
- HANDOFF.md is auto-generated at session end. You do NOT need to call
  write_session_handoff() — the runtime handles it automatically.
- If you want to record pending items mid-session, use write_scratchpad or
  write_file to HANDOFF.md directly.
- Auto-generates from git diff at session cleanup.

## Session Continuity
At the start of every session, HANDOFF.md from the previous session is injected.
- Read HANDOFF.md content carefully -- it tells you what was changed, what's pending,
  the plan progress, and the recent conversation from the last session.
- Use this to pick up where you left off without re-discovering context.
- At session end, HANDOFF.md is auto-generated with your changes, pending items,
  and recent user messages for continuity.

